    <!--
        THIS NOTE LIVES IN A COMMENT, NOT IN <description>, AND IT MUST STAY THAT WAY.
        RecordType.Description is capped at 255 characters. The full rationale below is
        operationally important, so it lives here rather than being trimmed away; a comment is
        repo-only and is never sent to the Metadata API. Do not paste this back into
        <description>. NOTE ALSO: the comment must sit INSIDE the root element - a comment
        placed ABOVE <RecordType> breaks `sf` deploy at source conversion with a misleading
        "unable to find matching parent xml file" error. The 255 cap has been breached THREE
        times in this programme; the lengths in this file were MEASURED with an XML parser.

        THE SELL-SIDE LOI. Parented by the new Disposition__c lookup. Five stages, ALL FIVE
        ADDED 2026-08-09 and none of them existing on Stage__c before this tranche:
        Received -> Under Review -> Countered by DPEG -> Counter Received from Buyer ->
        Executed (Part 2 lines 55-74).

        THE DIRECTION IS REVERSED, WHICH IS THE WHOLE REASON THIS RECORD TYPE EXISTS. On an
        acquisition DPEG DRAFTS the LOI and SENDS it out, so the path starts at Draft. On a
        disposition the LOI ARRIVES from the buyer through the appointed broker, so the path
        starts at Received and there is no Draft, no Prepare/Review and no Sent. The middle of
        the path is an explicit loop - we counter (Countered by DPEG), the buyer counters back
        (Counter Received from Buyer), "and it goes round until both sides are agreed" - which
        is why the disposition side has TWO counter values where the acquisition side has one.

        THE TWO STAGE SETS ARE FULLY DISJOINT, AND THAT IS THE LOAD-BEARING PROPERTY. No stage
        value appears on both record types, so every flexipage visibility rule keyed on a stage
        value is SELF-LIMITING to its own record type with no record-type criterion added.
        Contrast NDA__c, whose Sent and Signed ARE shared. If a future change makes any LOI
        stage shared, every such rule must be revisited.

        STEP ORDER IS ENFORCED BY THE MASTER VALUE SET, NOT BY THE PATH FILE. Runtime display
        order comes from Stage__c's picklist value order (sorted=false), filtered by this
        record type - a record type can only include or exclude, never re-order. The five
        values here were APPENDED after the five acquisition values, which renders this path as
        Received -> Under Review -> Countered by DPEG -> Counter Received from Buyer ->
        Executed AND leaves the acquisition rendering byte-identical to today's. Appending was
        sufficient only because the sets are disjoint; NDA__c needed an interleave (Not Sent
        moved ahead of Sent) because its sets overlap and an appended starting state rendered
        THIRD. Anyone re-ordering Stage__c must re-check BOTH record type files.

        THE MASTER DEFAULT IS Draft AND IT IS NOT REACHABLE HERE. Received carries
        default=true on this record type, which is what a new disposition LOI opens on. The
        field-level default (Draft) is untouched, because changing it would move the
        acquisition side's starting state - a live regression for zero benefit.

        WARNING - RECORD-TYPE PICKLIST RESTRICTION IS UI-ONLY. It is NOT enforced by Apex DML,
        so restricting Draft/Prepare/Review/Sent/Counter/Completed out here does not stop a
        direct write. The Apex-side allow-list in RecordStageAdvanceService must become
        record-type-aware in the same tranche; that is the parallel developer change.

        ACQUISITION-DIRECTIONAL FIELDS ARE LAYOUT-EXCLUDED, NOT DELETED. Submitted_Date__c,
        Approved_By__c, Approved_Date__c, Approval_Comments__c and LOI_Status__c stay on the
        object and keep their acquisition data; a separate disposition layout omits them. The
        Submit_for_Approval quick action needs no record-type criterion - it is gated on
        {!Record.Stage__c} EQUAL 'Prepare/Review', a value this record type does not carry - and
        Opportunity.LOI_Approval is an approval on the OPPORTUNITY object (entryCriteria
        StageName equals LOI) with no relationship to an LOI__c record, so it cannot fire here.

        LOI_Status__c IS STILL ENUMERATED IN FULL, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE RATHER THAN AN
        OVERSIGHT. It is acquisition vocabulary and is excluded from the disposition LAYOUT, but
        a record type cannot expose an empty picklist and, more importantly,
        Is_Advance_Allowed__c reads LOI_Status__c on EVERY LOI - its Draft default must remain
        available here or the formula's acquisition-only terms stop being inert. Note that
        CounterOfferService stamps LOI_Status__c = 'Countered' on every counter, including a
        disposition one; that is invisible on the disposition layout and is left alone
        deliberately, because suppressing it is a behaviour change nobody asked for.

        Ball_In_Court__c IS LEFT AT Us/Seller AND THAT IS AN OPEN ITEM, NOT A DECISION.
        On a sale the counterparty is the BUYER, so 'Seller' here would mean DPEG - the field
        does not merely read oddly, it INVERTS. Adding a Buyer value is raised as a
        recommendation and is deliberately not applied by this file, because it also requires
        CounterOfferService to choose the label from the parent's record type. Until that
        lands, Ball_In_Court__c on a disposition LOI must be read with care.

        =====================================================================
        🔴 RETRACTED 2026-08-10 (code review 3B/3C, W1 + W2). THE PARAGRAPH DIRECTLY ABOVE IS
        FALSE ON BOTH OF ITS CLAIMS. READ THIS INSTEAD.
        =====================================================================

        RETRACTED, verbatim: "Ball_In_Court__c IS LEFT AT Us/Seller AND THAT IS AN OPEN ITEM,
        NOT A DECISION... Adding a Buyer value is raised as a recommendation and is DELIBERATELY
        NOT APPLIED BY THIS FILE."

        BOTH HALVES WERE ALREADY UNTRUE WHEN THE REVIEW READ THEM:
          1. 'Buyer' IS APPLIED BY THIS FILE. It is in the Ball_In_Court__c block below and has
             been since 3B. The paragraph was written in an earlier pass and was never retracted
             when the decision landed later the same day.
          2. IT IS NO LONGER AN OPEN ITEM. The dependency the paragraph named as the blocker -
             "it also requires CounterOfferService to choose the label from the parent's record
             type" - WAS BUILT. CounterOfferService.isSaleSide resolves the side from the parent
             (record type first, Disposition__c lookup as the migration-window fallback) and
             stores a DERIVED label, never the caller's token, so a caller sending 'Seller' on a
             sale gets 'Buyer' stored. It is tested four ways.

        🔴 WHY THIS RETRACTION MATTERS MORE THAN ORDINARY COMMENT ROT, WHICH IS EXACTLY WHY THE
        REVIEW RAISED IT: this file is the stated authority for the token contract. A future
        reader who trusted the retracted paragraph would conclude that 'Buyer' was added by
        mistake, REMOVE IT - and silently break CounterOfferService's only sell-side write path,
        since the derived label would then have no value to land on. Do not remove 'Buyer'.

        =====================================================================
        W2 APPLIED IN THE SAME CHANGE: 'Seller' IS NOW RESTRICTED OUT OF Ball_In_Court__c HERE.
        =====================================================================

        The value set on this record type is now {Us, Buyer}, matching Disposition_PSA. The
        argument is that record type's, applied verbatim one object earlier: on a sale DPEG IS
        the seller, so 'Seller' would name THE SAME PARTY 'Us' ALREADY NAMES, and the field
        would carry no information at all. Leaving it exposed left a value whose only meaning
        was the inversion this record type exists to fix.

        ⚠ 'Seller' AS AN INCUMBENT WIRE TOKEN DOES NOT TRANSFER TO THIS FIELD, and that is the
        distinction to hold. Direction__c keeps 'Seller' accepted on both sides because clients
        SEND that token and a stored history of it exists. Ball_In_Court__c is never sent by any
        client - CounterOfferService derives it - so there is no incumbent to preserve.

        ESTABLISHED BEFORE REMOVING, NOT ASSUMED:
          - NO ROWS ARE AFFECTED. 3B is undeployed (D14), so no Disposition_LOI row exists in any
            org. Acquisition_LOI keeps 'Seller' and every live acquisition LOI is untouched. This
            is a record-type restriction, not a picklist-value removal, so the standing
            picklist-removal sweep rule is not triggered - the master value set is unchanged.
          - NO WRITER CAN PRODUCE IT. CounterOfferService is the only writer of this field and on
            a sale it writes 'Us' or 'Buyer' only. Restricting the value therefore closes the one
            remaining route in, which was a HAND EDIT or an INLINE EDIT - and the field is exposed
            for exactly that.
          - NO READER DEPENDS ON IT. No flexipage visibility rule on LOI_Record_Page references
            Ball_In_Court__c (they key on Stage__c, LOI_Status__c, Is_Advance_Allowed__c and the
            two driver flags), and Is_Advance_Allowed__c does not read it either.
        ⚠ Record-type picklist restriction remains UI-ONLY and is NOT enforced by Apex DML. This
        stops a USER picking 'Seller' on a sale; CounterOfferService is what stops CODE writing
        it, and that is where the enforcement actually lives.

        EVERY PICKLIST ON THE OBJECT IS ENUMERATED - Stage__c, LOI_Status__c and
        Ball_In_Court__c - because a record type file that omits a picklist silently drops all
        of its values from that type. Precedent:
        objects/Opportunity/recordTypes/Commercial.recordType-meta.xml. LOI__c has exactly
        three picklists; the fields/ directory was walked to establish that.

        No <businessProcess> - that element is for Opportunity, Case, Lead and Solution only.

        THE OFFER MIGRATION IS A SEPARATE, LATER WAVE AND IS NOT DONE BY THIS FILE. D6 retires
        the negotiation from Disposition_Offer__c (the counter values and the four counter
        price/date fields) so the offer becomes capture + comparison only. That half is a
        DESTRUCTIVE DATA MIGRATION which cannot begin until this record type is live and
        verified, and the standing picklist-removal sweep rule applies to it in full.

        POST-DEPLOY, NOT DEPLOYABLE: profile record-type assignment (System Administrator
        included), the per-profile DEFAULT, the disposition layout assignment, and confirming
        that a disposition persona creating an LOI from the related list sees NO record-type
        chooser - if one appears, the permission-set scoping is wrong. profiles/** is
        .forceignore'd in this repo.
    -->
